@startuml
' Hand-written EER diagram (categorical level). Implementation: shared-primary-key subtype tables.
!include ../../_style.iuml
allowmixing
title Categorical (EER) - People: generalization / specialization of Person

entity "Person\n(people.Persons)" as Person <<temporal>>
entity "Student\n(people.Students)" as Student
entity "Guardian\n(people.Guardians)" as Guardian
entity "Employee\n(people.Employees)" as Employee
entity "Teacher\n(people.Teachers)" as Teacher

usecase "o" as OverlapPerson
usecase "d" as DisjointEmployee

' Partial participation of the superclass (single line): a Person may hold no role
' (e.g. an applicant or a contact), so not every Person is specialised.
Person -- OverlapPerson : partial
OverlapPerson --> Student : subset
OverlapPerson --> Guardian : subset
OverlapPerson --> Employee : subset

' Teacher is a specialization of Employee (not a sibling of it).
Employee -- DisjointEmployee : partial
DisjointEmployee --> Teacher : subset

note right of OverlapPerson
  **o = OVERLAPPING** specialization of Person
  One person may be Student, Guardian and Employee at the
  same time (e.g. a teacher who is a parent of a student).
  Defining attribute: none - role = existence of subtype row.
  Participation: **PARTIAL** (persons with no role are valid:
  applicants, emergency contacts).
  Implemented by: PK of each subtype = FK to people.Persons.
  Roles view: people.vw_PersonRoles
end note

note right of DisjointEmployee
  Employee -> Teacher: single subclass, PARTIAL
  (accountants, registrars are employees but not teachers).
  Teachers.PersonId = PK and FK to people.Employees.
end note

legend bottom
  single line = partial participation, thick line = total participation
  o = overlapping, d = disjoint, subset = "is a subclass of"
endlegend
@enduml
